<Lesson>
  <Meta>
    <Id>lesson-01-03</Id>
    <Title>Hodgkin-Huxley Model: Biophysical Foundation</Title>
    <Version>1</Version>
    <Tags>
      <Tag>hodgkin-huxley</Tag>
      <Tag>ion-channels</Tag>
      <Tag>action-potentials</Tag>
      <Tag>gating-variables</Tag>
      <Tag>biophysics</Tag>
    </Tags>
  </Meta>

  <Section type="concept">
    <H1>Hodgkin-Huxley Model: Biophysical Foundation</H1>
    <Body>
Here's what won a Nobel Prize in 1963: a set of equations that predict *exactly* how a neuron fires, down to the millisecond and millivolt. Hodgkin and Huxley didn't have computers powerful enough to solve their own model — they used hand-cranked calculators for weeks — but their math was so precise that it matched experimental recordings from squid giant axons within measurement error.

The integrate-and-fire model you just learned treats the threshold as a mysterious boundary. Cross it, and boom — a spike. But *why* does that boundary exist? The Hodgkin-Huxley (HH) model answers this mechanistically: threshold emerges from the interplay of voltage-gated sodium and potassium channels, each with their own activation and inactivation dynamics. No abstraction, no hand-waving — just biophysics.

This lesson reveals how three gating variables — **m** (sodium activation), **h** (sodium inactivation), and **n** (potassium activation) — orchestrate the action potential through a precisely timed dance of opening and closing ion channels.
    </Body>
  </Section>

  <Section type="concept">
    <H2>Ionic Currents: The Foundation</H2>
    <Body>
Every ion channel is essentially a voltage-dependent resistor coupled to a battery. The battery is the **reversal potential** $E_{ion}$, determined by the concentration gradient across the membrane (remember the Nernst equation?). The resistor is the **conductance** $g_{ion}$, which changes with voltage as channels open and close.

The current flowing through a population of ion channels follows Ohm's law with a twist:

$$I_{ion} = g_{ion} \cdot (V - E_{ion})$$

Read this carefully: current is *proportional to the driving force* $(V - E_{ion})$. When the membrane potential $V$ equals the reversal potential $E_{ion}$, no current flows — even if every channel is wide open. This is why sodium current reverses direction during the late phase of an action potential.

For the HH model, we track three ionic currents:
- **Sodium current** $I_{Na}$: reversal potential around **+55 mV** (inward during depolarization)
- **Potassium current** $I_{K}$: reversal potential around **-77 mV** (outward during repolarization)
- **Leak current** $I_{L}$: reversal potential around **-54 mV** (mixed ion channels, mostly chloride)

The leak current is small but critical — it sets the resting potential by balancing the sodium and potassium leaks when voltage-gated channels are closed.
    </Body>
  </Section>

  <FlashCard id="fc-reversal-potential">
    <Front>What happens to ionic current when membrane voltage equals the reversal potential?</Front>
    <Back>Current becomes zero, regardless of conductance. The driving force (V - E_ion) disappears, so no net ion flow occurs even if channels are open.</Back>
  </FlashCard>

  <Section type="concept">
    <H2>Voltage-Gated Conductances</H2>
    <Body>
Here's where the model gets clever. Hodgkin and Huxley realized that conductance isn't constant — it depends on the fraction of channels in the open state. They modeled this with **gating variables** that range from 0 (all closed) to 1 (all open).

For sodium channels, they found two gates:
- **m**: activation gate (opens fast on depolarization)
- **h**: inactivation gate (closes slower, blocking the channel from inside)

A sodium channel conducts only when **m is open AND h is open**. Hodgkin and Huxley empirically determined that sodium conductance scales as:

$$g_{Na} = \bar{g}_{Na} \cdot m^3 \cdot h$$

Why $m^3$? They found that *three* independent activation gates must all be open simultaneously. Think of it like a triple-lock safe — miss one, and the channel stays shut.

Potassium channels are simpler: just one type of gate **n** (delayed activation), raised to the fourth power:

$$g_{K} = \bar{g}_{K} \cdot n^4$$

Four independent gates, all must open. The "delayed" part is crucial — potassium channels activate more slowly than sodium, allowing sodium to dominate early depolarization.
    </Body>
  </Section>

  <Mermaid>
graph TD
    A[Membrane at Rest] -->|Depolarizing stimulus| B[m gates open rapidly]
    B --> C[Sodium rushes in]
    C --> D[Voltage spikes toward +55 mV]
    D --> E[h gates close, n gates open]
    E --> F[Sodium blocked, potassium flows out]
    F --> G[Repolarization]
    G --> H[Hyperpolarization undershoot]
    H --> I[Gates reset to resting state]
</Mermaid>

  <FlashCard id="fc-gating-powers">
    <Front>Why is sodium conductance proportional to m³h instead of just m·h?</Front>
    <Back>Three independent m-type gates must all be open for the channel to conduct. The probability that all three are open is m × m × m = m³. This reflects the channel's molecular structure.</Back>
  </FlashCard>

  <Section type="concept">
    <H2>Gating Variable Dynamics</H2>
    <Body>
Each gating variable evolves according to first-order kinetics — a race between voltage-dependent opening and closing rates. The general form is:

$$\frac{dn}{dt} = \alpha_n(V) \cdot (1 - n) - \beta_n(V) \cdot n$$

Translation: $\alpha_n(V)$ is the *opening rate*, which tries to push $n$ toward 1. The term $(1 - n)$ represents the fraction of gates still closed (available to open). Meanwhile, $\beta_n(V)$ is the *closing rate*, trying to bring $n$ back to 0.

At steady state ($dn/dt = 0$), the gating variable settles at:

$$n_\infty(V) = \frac{\alpha_n(V)}{\alpha_n(V) + \beta_n(V)}$$

This is the **voltage-dependent equilibrium value**. At resting potential, $n_\infty$ is small (potassium channels mostly closed). At +20 mV, $n_\infty$ approaches 1 (channels mostly open).

The **time constant** of the transition is:

$$\tau_n(V) = \frac{1}{\alpha_n(V) + \beta_n(V)}$$

When $\tau_n$ is small, the gate responds quickly to voltage changes. When large, it lags behind. This is why **h** and **n** are called "slow" variables compared to **m**.
    </Body>
  </Section>

  <FlashCard id="fc-alpha-beta">
    <Front>What do α(V) and β(V) represent in the gating variable equations?</Front>
    <Back>α(V) is the voltage-dependent opening rate, β(V) is the closing rate. Together they determine both the steady-state value (n∞) and the time constant (τ) of the gating variable.</Back>
  </FlashCard>

  <Section type="concept">
    <H2>The Complete Hodgkin-Huxley Equation</H2>
    <Body>
Now we assemble the full model. The membrane capacitance $C$ integrates all currents (ionic + injected):

$$C \frac{dV}{dt} = -g_{Na} m^3 h (V - E_{Na}) - g_{K} n^4 (V - E_{K}) - g_{L}(V - E_{L}) + I_{ext}$$

This is the **HH voltage equation**. Combined with the three gating variable equations:

$$\frac{dm}{dt} = \alpha_m(V)(1 - m) - \beta_m(V) m$$
$$\frac{dh}{dt} = \alpha_h(V)(1 - h) - \beta_h(V) h$$
$$\frac{dn}{dt} = \alpha_n(V)(1 - n) - \beta_n(V) n$$

you have a **four-dimensional dynamical system**. The state is $(V, m, h, n)$, and each variable evolves according to voltage-dependent differential equations.

Hodgkin and Huxley fit the rate functions $\alpha$ and $\beta$ empirically to match voltage-clamp experiments on squid axons at 6.3°C. The functional forms are complicated (exponentials and rational functions), but they capture the biophysics beautifully. For example:

$$\alpha_m(V) = \frac{0.1(V + 40)}{1 - \exp\left(-\frac{V + 40}{10}\right)}$$

$$\beta_m(V) = 4 \exp\left(-\frac{V + 65}{18}\right)$$

These aren't arbitrary — they emerge from statistical mechanics of channel conformational changes. But for practical purposes, just know: **m responds to voltage in ~0.1 ms, h in ~1 ms, n in ~1-2 ms**.
    </Body>
  </Section>

